## an3.49
an3.49:1.1 | S: “In three situations, mendicants, you should be keen. | T: “In three instances, monks, ardency should be exercised.
an3.49:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.49:1.3 | S: You should be keen to prevent bad, unskillful qualities from arising. You should be keen to give rise to skillful qualities. And you should be keen to endure physical pain—sharp, severe, acute, unpleasant, disagreeable, life-threatening. | T: “Ardency should be exercised for the non-arising of unarisen evil, unskillful qualities. “Ardency should be exercised for the arising of unarisen skillful qualities. “Ardency should be exercised for enduring bodily feelings that have arisen and are painful, racking, sharp, piercing, disagreeable, displeasing, & menacing to life.
an3.49:1.4 | S: In these three situations, you should be keen. | T: (none)
an3.49:2.1 | S: It’s a mendicant who is keen to prevent bad, unskillful qualities from arising. They’re keen to give rise to skillful qualities. And they’re keen to endure physical pain—sharp, severe, acute, unpleasant, disagreeable, life-threatening. | T: “When one exercises ardency for the non-arising of unarisen evil, unskillful qualities, when one exercises ardency for the arising of unarisen skillful qualities, and when one exercises ardency for enduring bodily feelings that have arisen and are painful, racking, sharp, piercing, disagreeable, displeasing, & menacing to life,
an3.49:2.2 | S: This is called a mendicant who is keen, alert, and mindful so as to rightly make an end of suffering.” | T: then one is said to be a monk who is ardent, adept, & mindful for the right ending of suffering & stress.”
an3.49:2.3 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti23
iti23:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti23:2.1 | S: “This one thing, mendicants, when developed and cultivated, secures equal benefits for both | T: “This one quality, monks, when developed & pursued, keeps both kinds of benefit secure:
iti23:2.2 | S: this life and lives to come. | T: benefit in this life & in lives to come.
iti23:2.3 | S: What one thing? | T: Which one quality?
iti23:2.4 | S: Diligence in skillful qualities. | T: Heedfulness with regard to skillful qualities.
iti23:2.5 | S: This is the one thing that, when developed and cultivated, secures equal benefits for both | T: This is the one quality that, when developed & pursued, keeps both kinds of benefit secure:
iti23:2.6 | S: this life and lives to come.” | T: benefit in this life & in lives to come.”
iti23:2.7 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti23:2.8 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti23:3.1 | S: “The astute praise diligence | T: They praise heedfulness, the wise,
iti23:3.2 | S: in making merit. | T: in doing acts of merit.
iti23:3.3 | S: Being diligent, an astute person | T: When heedful, wise,
iti23:3.4 | S: secures both benefits: | T: you achieve both kinds of benefit:
iti23:4.1 | S: the benefit in this life, | T: benefits in this life,
iti23:4.2 | S: and in lives to come. | T: & benefits in lives to come.
iti23:4.3 | S: Attentive, comprehending the meaning, | T: By breaking through to your benefit, you’re called enlightened,
iti23:4.4 | S: they are said to be astute.” | T: wise.
iti23:5.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti23:5.2 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti40
iti40:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti40:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, ignorance leads the attainment of unskillful qualities, with lack of conscience and prudence following along. | T: “Monks, ignorance precedes the arrival of unskillful qualities; lack of shame & lack of compunction follow after.
iti40:2.2 | S: Knowledge leads the attainment of skillful qualities, with conscience and prudence following along.” | T: Clear knowing precedes the arrival of skillful qualities; shame & compunction follow after.”
iti40:2.3 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti40:2.4 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti40:3.1 | S: “Bad destinies of whatever kind, | T: Any bad destinations
iti40:3.2 | S: in this world or the next, | T: in this world, in the next,
iti40:3.3 | S: are all rooted in ignorance, | T: are rooted in ignorance–all–
iti40:3.4 | S: compounded of greed and desire. | T: accumulations of desire & greed.
iti40:4.1 | S: Since one of corrupt wishes is | T: And when a person of evil desires
iti40:4.2 | S: unconscientious, lacking regard for others, | T: lacks shame & respect,
iti40:4.3 | S: they brim with much wickedness, | T: evil comes from that,
iti40:4.4 | S: which sends them to a place of loss. | T: and by that he goes to deprivation.
iti40:5.1 | S: Therefore, dispelling desire | T: So cleansing away
iti40:5.2 | S: and greed and ignorance, | T: ignorance, desire, & greed,
iti40:5.3 | S: a mendicant arousing knowledge | T: a monk giving rise to clear knowing
iti40:5.4 | S: would abandon all bad destinies.” | T: would abandon all bad destinations.
iti40:6.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti40:6.2 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)
iti40:6.3 | P: Paṭhamabhāṇavāro. | T: (none)

## iti42
iti42:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti42:2.1 | S: “These two bright things, mendicants, protect the world. | T: “Monks, these two bright qualities safeguard the world.
iti42:2.2 | S: What two? | T: Which two?
iti42:2.3 | S: Conscience and prudence. | T: Shame & compunction.
iti42:2.4 | S: If these two bright things did not protect the world, there would be no recognition of the status of mother, aunts, or wives and partners of tutors and respected people. | T: If these two bright qualities did not safeguard the world, there would be no discerning of “mother,” “aunt,” “uncle’s wife,” “teacher’s wife,” or “wife of those deserving respect.”
iti42:2.5 | S: The world would become dissolute, like goats and sheep, chickens and pigs, and hounds and jackals. | T: The world would fall into promiscuity, like rams with goats, roosters with pigs, or dogs with jackals.
iti42:2.6 | S: But because the two bright things protect the world, there is recognition of the status of mother, aunts, and wives and partners of tutors and respected people.” | T: But because these two bright qualities do safeguard the world, there is the discerning of “mother,” “aunt,” “uncle’s wife,” “teacher’s wife,” & “wife of those deserving respect.”
iti42:2.7 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti42:2.8 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti42:3.1 | S: “Those in whom conscience and prudence | T: Those in whom shame & compunction
iti42:3.2 | S: are never found at all, | T: are not always found
iti42:3.3 | S: have lost their bright roots, | T: have strayed from the bright root,
iti42:3.4 | S: and fare on in birth and death. | T: are headed to birth & death.
iti42:4.1 | S: Those in whom conscience and prudence | T: But those in whom shame & compunction
iti42:4.2 | S: are always rightly established, | T: always are rightly established,
iti42:4.3 | S: thrive in the spiritual life; | T: who are mature in the holy life:
iti42:4.4 | S: being at peace, they will not be reborn again.” | T: they are calm, their further becoming ended.
iti42:5.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti42:5.2 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti51
iti51:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti51:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three elements. | T: “Monks, there are these three properties.
iti51:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
iti51:2.3 | S: The realm of luminous form, the formless realm, and cessation. | T: The property of form, the property of formlessness, the property of cessation.
iti51:2.4 | S: These are the three elements.” | T: These are the three properties.”
iti51:2.5 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti51:2.6 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti51:3.1 | S: “Comprehending the realm of luminous form, | T: Comprehending the property of form,
iti51:3.2 | S: not stuck in the formless, | T: not taking a stance in the formless,
iti51:3.3 | S: those who are released in cessation, | T: those released in cessation
iti51:3.4 | S: they are conquerors of death. | T: are people who’ve left death behind.
iti51:4.1 | S: Having directly experienced <j>the element free of death, | T: Having touched with his body the deathless property free
iti51:4.2 | S: free of attachments; | T: from acquisitions,
iti51:4.3 | S: having realized relinquishment | T: having realized the relinquishing
iti51:4.4 | S: of attachments, the undefiled | T: of acquisitions, effluent-free,
iti51:4.5 | S: fully awakened Buddha teaches | T: the Rightly Self-awakened One teaches the state
iti51:4.6 | S: the sorrowless, stainless state.” | T: with no sorrow, no dust.
iti51:5.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti51:5.2 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti61
iti61:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti61:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three eyes. | T: “Monks, there are these three eyes.
iti61:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
iti61:2.3 | S: The eye of the flesh, the eye of clairvoyance, and the eye of wisdom. | T: The eye of flesh, the divine eye, & the eye of discernment.
iti61:2.4 | S: These are the three eyes.” | T: These are the three eyes.”
iti61:2.5 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti61:2.6 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti61:3.1 | S: “The eye of flesh, the clairvoyant eye, | T: The eye of flesh, the eye divine,
iti61:3.2 | S: the supreme eye of wisdom: | T: the eye of discernment unsurpassed:
iti61:3.3 | S: these three eyes | T: these three eyes were taught
iti61:3.4 | S: were taught by the supreme person. | T: by the Superlative Person.
iti61:4.1 | S: The arising of the eye of flesh | T: The arising of the eye of flesh
iti61:4.2 | S: is the path to the clairvoyant eye. | T: is the path to the eye divine.
iti61:4.3 | S: When knowledge arises— | T: When knowledge arises,
iti61:4.4 | S: the supreme eye of wisdom— | T: the eye of discernment unsurpassed:
iti61:4.5 | S: one who gains that eye | T: whoever gains this eye
iti61:4.6 | S: is released from all suffering.” | T: is–from all suffering & stress– released.
iti61:5.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti61:5.2 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti78
iti78:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti78:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, sentient beings join together and converge because of an element: | T: “Monks, it’s in accordance with their properties that beings come together & associate with one another.
iti78:2.2 | S: Those who have inferior convictions join together and converge with those who have inferior convictions. | T: Beings of low dispositions come together & associate with beings of low dispositions.
iti78:2.3 | S: Those who have good convictions join together and converge with those who have good convictions. | T: Beings of admirable dispositions come together & associate with beings of admirable dispositions.
iti78:3.1 | S: In the past … | T: In the past, it was in accordance with their properties that beings came together & associated with one another.…
iti78:3.2 | P: Hīnādhimuttikā sattā hīnādhimuttikehi sattehi saddhiṁ saṁsandiṁsu samiṁsu, | T: (none)
iti78:3.3 | P: kalyāṇādhimuttikā sattā kalyāṇādhimuttikehi sattehi saddhiṁ saṁsandiṁsu samiṁsu. | T: (none)
iti78:4.1 | S: In the future … | T: In the future, it will be in accordance with their properties that beings will come together & associate with one another.…
iti78:4.2 | P: Hīnādhimuttikā sattā hīnādhimuttikehi sattehi saddhiṁ saṁsandissanti samessanti, | T: (none)
iti78:4.3 | P: kalyāṇādhimuttikā sattā kalyāṇādhimuttikehi sattehi saddhiṁ saṁsandissanti samessanti. | T: (none)
iti78:5.1 | S: At present, too, sentient beings join together and converge because of an element. | T: And now at present, it’s in accordance with their properties that beings come together & associate with one another.
iti78:5.2 | S: Those who have inferior convictions join together and converge with those who have inferior convictions. | T: Beings of low dispositions come together & associate with beings of low dispositions.
iti78:5.3 | S: Those who have good convictions join together and converge with those who have good convictions.” | T: Beings of admirable dispositions come together & associate with beings of admirable dispositions.”
iti78:5.4 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti78:5.5 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti78:6.1 | S: “Socializing creates a snarl; | T: The underbrush born of association
iti78:6.2 | S: they’re cut off by being aloof. | T: is cut away by non-association.
iti78:6.3 | S: If you’re lost in the middle of a great sea, | T: Just as one riding a small wooden plank would sink in the great sea,
iti78:6.4 | S: and you clamber up on a little log, you’ll sink. | T: (none)
iti78:7.1 | S: So too, a person who lives well | T: so does even one of right living
iti78:7.2 | S: sinks by relying on a lazy person. | T: sink, associating with the lazy.
iti78:7.3 | S: Hence you should avoid such | T: So avoid the lazy,
iti78:7.4 | S: a lazy person who lacks energy. | T: those with low persistence.
iti78:8.1 | S: Dwell with the noble ones | T: Live with the noble ones–
iti78:8.2 | S: who are secluded and determined | T: secluded, resolute, absorbed in jhāna,
iti78:8.3 | S: constantly energetic, | T: their persistence constantly aroused
iti78:8.4 | S: the astute who practice absorption.” | T: : the wise.
iti78:9.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti78:9.2 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti85
iti85:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti85:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, meditate observing the ugliness of the body. | T: “Remain focused, monks, on foulness in the body.
iti85:2.2 | S: Let mindfulness of breathing be well-established internally on the present. | T: Have mindfulness of in-&-out breathing well-established to the fore within you.
iti85:2.3 | S: Meditate observing the impermanence of all conditions. | T: Remain focused on the inconstancy of all fabrications.
iti85:2.4 | S: As you meditate observing the ugliness of the body, you will give up desire for the body. | T: For one who remains focused on the foulness of the body, the obsession with passion for the property of beauty is abandoned.
iti85:2.5 | S: When mindfulness of breathing is well-established internally on the present, there will be no distressing external thoughts or wishes. | T: For one who has mindfulness of in-&-out breathing well-established to the fore within oneself, annoying external thoughts & inclinations don’t exist.

